@* How to play: the game's ideas, aims and scoring, for a player rather than an engine (that is rules.md).
   Shown over the lobby or the table, from the page's settings, until closed. *@

<div class="guide-backdrop">
    <section class="guide" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-labelledby="guide-title">
        <header class="guide-head">
            <h2 id="guide-title">How to play</h2>
            <button class="close" @onclick="this.OnClose">Close</button>
        </header>

        <h3>The aim</h3>
        <p>
            Four armies play, in two teams: allies sit opposite each other. There is no checkmate. The game ends
            either when one team has no pieces left, and the other wins outright; or when everyone agrees to end
            it, and then points decide: taking pieces scores them, and a few bonuses more (below).
        </p>

        <h3>Your army</h3>
        <p>
            Each army has a Raja (the king), an Elephant (rook), a Horse (knight), a Boat and four Pawns. The square your Raja
            starts on is your <strong>throne</strong>. Each army's Pawns move toward the middle of the board, so
            "forward" is different for each seat.
        </p>
        <table class="guide-pieces">
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Piece</th><th>Moves</th><th>Taking it scores</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>Raja</td><td>One square in any direction.</td><td>5</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Elephant</td><td>Any distance straight along a row or column, like a chess rook.</td><td>4</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Horse</td><td>Like a chess knight, jumping.</td><td>3</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Boat</td><td>Exactly two squares diagonally, jumping over the square between.</td><td>2</td></tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Pawn</td>
                    <td>
                        One square forward; it takes one square diagonally forward. Two allied Pawns back to back,
                        each facing the other, may swap places, as a Pawn's move.
                    </td>
                    <td>1</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>Every piece but the Pawn takes the way it moves, by landing on an enemy.</p>

        <h3>Your turn</h3>
        <p>On your turn, you do one of these:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Move</strong> any one of your pieces, without rolling.</li>
            <li>
                <strong>Roll</strong> two dice. Each die lets you move one piece of the kind it shows: 1 a Boat,
                2 a Horse, 3 or 4 an Elephant, 5 or 6 a Pawn or the Raja. Use both, one or neither, in either
                order. A die nothing can use is lost.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Pass</strong>.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Nothing is ever compulsory. The very first turn of the game is the roll-off's winner playing the die
            that won it.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>Doubles</strong> let you make two moves with the kind they show (with one piece or two), or,
            instead, bring a captured piece of that kind back home: it <strong>escapes</strong>, taking any enemy
            on its square, and your turn ends. <strong>Beware:</strong> an escape takes whatever enemy stands on
            that square, even a Raja on its own throne, and scores for it as any capture does.
        </p>

        <h3>Points</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Taking a piece scores its points, which you keep even if the piece comes back later.</li>
            <li><strong>+1</strong> each time your Raja enters an enemy's throne.</li>
            <li><strong>+2</strong> for taking an enemy Raja on its own throne.</li>
            <li>
                <strong>+1</strong> for each prisoner you send home in a ransom you accept, or <strong>+2</strong>
                for a Raja. Prisoners given back to you score nothing.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>The empire:</strong> when the last enemy Raja is taken, each player of the team that took it
                who commands an ally's army (see below) scores <strong>+1</strong>. If the one who took it commands
                an ally's army, they score <strong>+2</strong> more, or <strong>+4</strong> if they took it on its
                throne. It can happen again, if an enemy Raja comes back and is taken again.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Farming:</strong> stepping your Raja off an enemy throne and back on, again and again,
                scores each entry, but those points are at risk until you do something else (move another
                piece, or step off to attack or defend one). If your Raja is taken meanwhile, you lose them, and
                its captor scores twice as much, besides the Raja's 5. (If your own team takes it, with an enemy
                army it commands, no one scores them.)
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>+2</strong> when, on your turn, you command every piece on the board. It is scored once,
                and not again until all four players have pieces on the board again.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Thrones and command</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>
                While your Raja sits on another seat's throne, ally's or enemy's, you may move that army as well as
                your own on your turns. While your ally has no Raja on the board, you may move their army too.
                Either way, you command that army.
            </li>
            <li>
                While your ally's Raja sits on your throne, or you have no Raja, your ally may take your turn for
                you, if you hand it over ("Let ... take over").
            </li>
            <li>
                While an enemy Raja sits on your throne, you must roll. You make the first move; the enemy makes the
                second, with your army, unless your first move took that Raja. If the enemy passes on it, the move is
                yours again.
            </li>
            <li>
                While an enemy holds your throne, or your ally's, any piece you move may take that army's
                pieces, to protect yourself from them. Your <strong>safety</strong> (under your corner of the board,
                on unless you turn it off) keeps you from taking your own team's pieces. Taking them scores no one.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Prisoners</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>
                The pieces you take are your prisoners. A Pawn reaching the far edge may become any captured piece,
                now or on a later turn.
            </li>
            <li>
                On your turn you may offer another player a <strong>ransom</strong>: a trade of prisoners, any
                number for any number. It happens only if they accept. Pieces given back return as near their
                starting squares as they can, never taking anything. Accepting scores you a point for each
                prisoner you send home, 2 for a Raja (see Points).
            </li>
            <li>
                Prisoners of your own team (taken while an enemy held their throne) you <strong>release</strong>
                instead, the same way: yours go home at once, and so do your ally's, unless an enemy holds your
                ally's throne: then your ally must accept. You may also offer your ally a trade, asking for your
                own pieces they hold, which they must accept; it scores no one. A piece so taken must stay prisoner
                until every player has had a turn. Going home, it takes nothing.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h3>The end</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>A team wins outright when the other team has no pieces left.</li>
            <li>
                Or, before that, anyone may press <strong>End game</strong> to ask the others to end it. If everyone agrees, points
                decide, whatever the margin; if anyone refuses, play goes on. A player may also give up their turns as
                they ask.
            </li>
            <li>
                No one is ever made to stop: as long as someone on each team wants to keep going, the game goes on.
                A team that is behind may always concede, by everyone on it giving up their turns: the game ends,
                and the other team wins. A team that is ahead may end it that way only with a clear lead: at least
                10 points more, and at least 10 points more in pieces left on the board, counted as they would score
                if taken (an Elephant, a Raja and a Pawn, say). Otherwise the other team plays on, and the game ends
                if the team that gave up falls behind.
            </li>
            <li>If only Rajas are left, it is a draw.</li>
            <li>
                A player who loses every piece sits out while their ally plays on alone, until a piece of theirs
                comes back, by ransom or by escaping.
            </li>
            <li>
                A player left with only their Raja may <strong>retire</strong> at the start of their turn. Their
                turns are skipped from then on, and taking their Raja scores no one.
            </li>
            <li>If three whole rounds go by with everyone passing, the game ends, and points decide.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>More</h3>
        <p class="more">
            Every rule in full: <a href="@DocsLinks.Rules" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the rules</a>. Playing
            with an AI of your own, or writing one:
            <a href="@DocsLinks.Bots" target="_blank" rel="noopener">bring your own AI</a>. What is kept about
            you, and how to have it deleted: <a href="@DocsLinks.Privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">privacy</a>.
        </p>
    </section>
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public EventCallback OnClose { get; set; }
}
